WRITE-UP (3-5 LINES)
------------------------------------------------------------ 

1. Best model: Decision Tree, Information Gain (R) (F1 = 0.7562, AUC = 0.7750 on balanced test set).
2. All three R models perform similarly (~73-74% accuracy), suggesting
   that the signal in these 21 survey features has a natural ceiling.
   KNN achieved the highest AUC, likely because it captures non-linear
   local patterns missed by single decision trees.
3. On realistic-prevalence data (14% diabetes), recall at the default
   0.5 threshold drops below 11%, because the model rarely predicts the
   rare class. Lowering the threshold to 0.2 raises recall to ~61% but
   precision falls to ~32%. A screening tool must accept this trade-off.
4. Association rules confirm that High BP + High Cholesterol + Obesity
   + Fair/Poor health = very strong diabetes signal (lift ~1.7).

------------------------------------------------------------ 
LIMITATIONS
------------------------------------------------------------ 

- The balanced 50/50 sample does not reflect population prevalence.
- Prediabetes (4,631 respondents) is coded 0 in the binary target.
- All data is self-reported from a single cross-sectional survey (2015).
- Associations do not imply causation.

